# gitleaks fingerprints: `<commit>:<file>:<rule-id>:<line>`.
#
# A fingerprint is the narrowest suppression gitleaks offers — it names one
# finding in one commit — so this is where a one-off historical false positive
# goes. A recurring SHAPE (a placeholder value, a test-mode provider key) gets a
# content-scoped allowlist in `.gitleaks.toml` instead, so the next test that
# uses the same fixture does not turn the required check red.
#
# To add one: run the scan the `Secret scan (gitleaks)` job runs, take the
# `Fingerprint` field from the JSON report, and paste it here with a comment
# saying what the value is and why it is not a secret. `docs/SECURITY.md` →
# "Secret scanning" has the full command.
#
# WHEN YOU WRITE THAT COMMENT, DESCRIBE THE VALUE — NEVER PASTE IT.
# gitleaks scans this file like any other, so a comment that reproduces the
# suppressed value in its original `NAME=value` or `key: "value"` shape becomes a
# NEW finding, in a new commit, which this file then has to suppress as well. The
# first draft of these annotations did exactly that and added four findings to a
# branch whose whole purpose was to make the scan blocking; the branch was
# rewritten before it was pushed rather than pinning the self-inflicted four.
#
# EVERY ENTRY BELOW WAS RE-VERIFIED FOR #2686 against gitleaks v8.28.0 on the
# tree at 66448740c. The ten entries this file used to hold were all stale: they
# named PRE-SQUASH pull-request commits (`7922b5219515…`, `1ec776ee3aa6…`,
# `81c6a66125a3…`, `321ac3277e02…`), which exist only under `refs/pull/*/head`
# on GitHub and are reachable from no branch or tag. Neither a normal clone nor
# `actions/checkout` with `fetch-depth: 0` ever fetches them, so those
# fingerprints suppressed nothing in any scan that has ever run. Where the same
# content survived the squash onto `main`, it is re-pinned below at its real
# commit — `.github/workflows/ci.yml` is now covered by the repeating-hex
# allowlist, and the `member-credit` idempotency keys by the UUID allowlist.

